import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base
  title="Open Questions"
  eyebrow="The research queue, in priority order"
  dek="What this archive does not know, ranked by how much finding out would change. A null result is still a result, and it stops the next person repeating the search.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Top of the queue, 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Where was Hannah Saniger born?</h2>

    <p class="lede">This is the oldest open question on the site and the only one left in the English
    line. Everything known about it is a negative: <strong>not Gloucestershire</strong> (the 1841
    census ticks the column), <strong>not any of the 81 Gloucestershire Saniger households</strong>
    (the reconstitution excludes her), and <strong>not stated on the ship</strong> (the register
    dittoed her husband's county over hers).</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>It now has a candidate, and the candidate is in Somerset.</strong> The 1851 census
      finds a Saniger household at Oxford Road in St Philip and Jacob — the Bristol parish adjoining
      St Paul, where the Wakefields were living — headed by a journeyman smith born at
      <strong>Chew Magna, Somerset</strong>, with his widowed mother, also born at Chew Magna,
      in the house. Right county, right generation, right trade, two streets away.
      <br /><br />
      FreeREG's Chew Magna transcript is a fragment — two Smith entries in twenty-one years — so it
      cannot be tested from a desk. The register itself is at Taunton and on film.
      <a href={u("/chew-magna")}>The whole argument, and the control test →</a>
      <span class="chip inf">a lead, not a link</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Where it stands, 10 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The state of play</h2>
    <p class="lede">Two days of work have closed more of this list than they opened, which is not the
    usual direction. What follows is the honest position, including the searches that found nothing.</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.3rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip dis">new, and it outranks everything</span><br />
        The parish registers of Berkeley, Gloucestershire</h3>
        <p>The deepest line in this archive — six Saniger generations from <strong>1654 to 1805</strong> —
        rested entirely on <strong>other people's family trees</strong>.
        <strong>Attempted 10 September 2026.</strong> FreeREG's Berkeley transcript runs only to
        <strong>1677</strong>, so it can test one generation of six — and it gives a good candidate
        plus a father the archive never had, <strong>Edward Sainger of Wanswell</strong>. The other
        five are untouched, and the surname turns out to be six times commoner at Dursley and Cam.
        <strong>What is needed now is Gloucestershire Archives</strong>: Berkeley St Mary after 1677,
        Dursley St James, Cam St George. <a href={u("/berkeley")}>What the register said →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The one document that would change most</h3>
        <p><strong><span class="mono">PROB 11/1728/64</span> — Robert D'Arcy's own will</strong>,
        proved 4 July 1827, <strong>digitised</strong>, about £3.50. Seventy-one of the direct line's
        ancestors and twelve of its generations hang on the join immediately above him, and nothing
        in his life names a father. A will names family. <a href={u("/hornby")}>Why →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Blocked on a card, not on research</h3>
        <p>Four Queensland <strong>death certificates</strong> with their registration numbers in
        hand; <strong>nine QSA items</strong>, all identified to series and file number, none
        digitised; and <strong>nine drafted letters</strong>, none sent. Every one of these is
        located. None can be bought by a machine.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Blocked on somebody else's website</h3>
        <p><strong>Three institutions are between houses.</strong> The <strong>CWGC</strong> has
        refused automated access twice. The <strong>Australian War Memorial</strong> is mid-migration
        and its search returns nothing for any query — still true on 10 September 2026; its documents
        were reached by direct URL instead. And <strong>NSW State Archives has become Museums of
        History NSW</strong>, at a new domain, with its old index addresses dead — which is what
        stopped the search for George Pitt D'Arcy's 1849 probate. All three are waiting on somebody
        else's rebuild, not on research.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Searched, and genuinely empty</h3>
        <p>The 4th Earl of Holderness's will is not in the Prerogative Court. Samuel Charles Sneyd is
        in no Blue Book from 1870. There is no English baptism for Robert D'Arcy. The
        <em>London Gazette</em> does not name Vivian Sneyd's Military Medal. Each is recorded below
        as a result, not a gap.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">1 · The join that decides the whole pedigree</h2>
    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip lore">searched 9 Sep 2026 — not found</span><br />Robert D'Arcy's baptism, c. 1750–51</h3>
        <p>Swept on FindMyPast for a Robert D'Arcy/Darcy baptised c. 1741–1761, name variants on,
        all English record sets: <strong>no result</strong>. His burial <em>is</em> now confirmed —
        St Mary, Chatham, 19 May 1827, aged 76 — which is where the famous "1751" actually comes
        from, and it names no father. Any further search must go to unindexed originals.
        <a href={u("/hornby")}>Why it matters →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip doc">searched 9 Sep 2026 — found, and it answers everything but the question</span><br />Connolly's <em>Roll of Officers of the Corps of Royal Engineers</em></h3>
        <p>Entry <strong>203</strong>: "D'Arcy, Robert, C.B." — second lieutenant 17 January 1776
        through major-general 12 August 1819, war services, and "Died at Chatham, 13 5 1827". Only
        <strong>two D'Arcys in the whole Roll</strong>, 1660–1898; the other is his son Constantine.
        <strong>It records no parentage at all</strong> — the Roll does not carry fathers.
        <a href={u("/hornby")}>The full entry →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip dis">ruled out 9 Sep 2026</span><br />The Woolwich cadet registers</h3>
        <p>Named here last as the best remaining hope, and <strong>wrong</strong>. <strong>WO 149</strong>
        runs from <strong>1790</strong> and is held at Sandhurst — it cannot contain a cadet of the
        early 1770s. The printed <em>Records of the Royal Military Academy</em>, 1851 and 1892, have
        also been searched in full: no Robert D'Arcy. They are institutional histories, not registers.
        Both closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip inf">now the best hope</span><br />Board of Ordnance records — WO 47, WO 54</h3>
        <p>Until 1855 the Royal Engineers answered to the <strong>Board of Ordnance</strong>, not the
        War Office, and kept separate records. If a cadet warrant or commission entry of
        <strong>17 January 1776</strong> survives anywhere, it is in the Ordnance minutes or registers.
        A letter to the Royal Engineers Museum at Chatham, whose whole subject is this Corps, is
        drafted and waiting.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>His Royal Engineers commission papers</h3>
        <p>Cadet and commission records of the 1760s–70s at the National Archives frequently name a
        parent or a patron. For an illegitimate son of a peer, a patron of the right surname would be
        the strongest indirect evidence obtainable. Note that he has <strong>no</strong> entry in the
        <em>Dictionary of National Biography</em> — the DNB's "D'Arcy, Robert" is the 4th Earl.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3><span class="chip doc">found 9 Sep 2026 — and it is his own</span><br />
        <span class="mono">PROB 11/1728/64</span> — the will of Robert D'Arcy himself</h3>
        <p>Searching the National Archives for the <em>Earl's</em> will turned up something better:
        <strong>"Will of Robert D'Arcy, Major General in the Army and Colonel of the Royal Engineers
        of Chatham, Kent"</strong>, proved <strong>4 July 1827</strong>. Died at Chatham that May,
        Major-General, Colonel of the Corps — it is unmistakably him, and
        <strong>it is digitised</strong>. A man's own will names his family. This is now the best
        unread document in the archive and it costs £3.50.
        <a href={u("/hornby")}>Why it matters →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Resolve the tree's self-contradiction</h3>
        <p>The note says 4th Earl; the structure descends through a "Thomas (Francis) D'Arcy" from the
        3rd. Establish which claim came first and who made it — a nineteenth-century family bible, a
        Victorian county history, or a twentieth-century online tree. Provenance would tell us a great
        deal about how much weight it deserves.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>2 · The Australian arrival</h2>
    <ul class="dek">
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered</span> <strong>Which ship?</strong> The convict ship
      <strong><em>England</em></strong>. He embarked with a detachment of thirty men of the 39th on
      8 April 1826; the pilot was discharged in the Downs on 6 May; she reached Port Jackson on
      18 September after 135 days with no deaths among her 148 convicts. <strong>"Mrs. D'Arcy and
      family"</strong> were cabin passengers. <a href={u("/australia")}>The voyage →</a></li>
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered</span> <strong>Which Maria?</strong> Neither marriage was
      at Chatham in 1810. He married <strong>Mary Ludlam in Kent in 1806</strong> — <em>Le
      Mesurier</em> was her mother's maiden name, which is how it got into the tree — and
      <strong>Maria White in County Wicklow about 1819</strong>. So Frederick Robert the surveyor is
      a <strong>half-brother</strong> to our line. <a href={u("/australia")}>Set out here →</a></li>
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered</span> <strong>The first marriage is dated, and it is
      1805.</strong> The <em>Salisbury and Winchester Journal</em> of 23 September 1805, p. 3:
      "<strong>Captain George d'Arcy</strong>", son of "<strong>Lieut Colonel d'Arcy</strong>",
      married "<strong>Miss Ludlam</strong>". A contemporary printing that also ties him to his
      father. <a href={u("/australia")}>Here →</a></li>
      <li><strong>Still to do:</strong> the c.1819 <strong>County Wicklow</strong> marriage to Maria
      White, still known only at second hand — and the 1805 parish entry behind the newspaper
      notice.</li>
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered</span> <strong>George Lindsay D'Arcy's christening.</strong>
      Indexed from the register: <strong>St James Sydney, 5 November 1829</strong>, father
      <strong>George Pitt Darcy</strong>, mother <strong>Maria</strong> — which proves Maria White,
      not Mary Ludlam, was his mother, and makes Frederick Robert a half-brother.</li>
      <li><strong>How old was the Major?</strong> Every 1849 death notice says <strong>69</strong>,
      giving a birth about 1780; the tree says 18 February 1783; WikiTree offers "about 1781". Three
      answers, no register. Try the 39th's muster rolls and his commission papers.</li>
      <li><strong>Where was he between 1832 and 1849?</strong> His regiment left for India in 1832
      and he did not go. Did he sell out, retire on half pay, or transfer? Half-pay lists would say —
      and his obituary's account of a decline "not passed in the paths of pleasantness" suggests the
      answer matters.</li>
      <li><strong>The Orthes gold medal.</strong> It was sent in error to Lieutenant-Colonel Bruce and
      took four years to reach him. The correspondence would be in the War Office papers, and it
      would be worth reading.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>3 · The Brisbane generations</h2>
    <ul class="dek">
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered in part</span> <strong>Lindesay Atkinson D'Arcy's
      service.</strong> <strong>Sergeant 11335</strong>, 12th Australian Depot Unit of Supply,
      Australian Army Service Corps; enlisted Brisbane 13 December 1915; embarked HMAT
      <em>Demosthenes</em> A64; returned to Australia 7 July 1919; next of kin his wife Ivy Miriam.
      <strong>Searched at RecordSearch, 9 September 2026, and not found.</strong> No
      <span class="mono">B2455</span> dossier exists for him under <em>Lindesay Atkinson</em> or
      <em>Lindsay Atkinson</em>, or in the World War I name index under <em>D'Arcy</em> or
      <em>Darcy</em>. The method was checked against his brother-in-law's record, which came back at
      once. The claim that a fourteen-page file "is known to exist" is withdrawn.
      <strong>Still to do:</strong> ask the National Reference Service, since NAA warns that not all
      items in the series are catalogued online — and order
      <span class="mono">NAA: J34, C34558</span>, the <strong>repatriation pension case file</strong>
      opened for his widow in 1937, which the same search turned up and which is not digitised.
      <a href={u("/great-war")}>His service →</a></li>
      <li><span class="chip doc">largely answered</span> <strong>Arthur Hartley Sneyd,
      Private 5050, 9th Battalion.</strong> His <strong>thirty-page B2455 dossier has now been read</strong>:
      attested Brisbane 16 September 1915, a typist of 5 ft 7½ in, made corporal then sergeant, sent to
      Étaples in April 1916, and <em>reverted to the ranks on joining the 9th Battalion on 29 July
      1916</em> — twenty-two days before he was killed. The report of death records his burial place
      as "<em>Not yet to hand</em>", and his effects — <em>a scarf and letters</em> — were shipped on
      the <em>Barunga</em>, which was torpedoed twenty-five days later.
      His <strong>Red Cross Wounded and Missing file</strong> has also been read: he survived the
      shell that was thought to have killed him, was seen the next morning with his head bandaged
      carrying a white flag behind stretcher-bearers, and was buried a year later, on report, in a
      shell hole in No Man's Land near Mouquet Farm under a rough wooden cross.
      <a href={u("/finding-arthur")}>The search →</a>
      <br /><strong>Still to do:</strong> the <strong>CWGC</strong> confirmation that he is named on
      the Villers-Bretonneux memorial — their site has now blocked automated access twice, so the
      commemoration stands as <span class="chip inf">inferred</span>. And one unresolved conflict:
      every official record says he died on <strong>20 August 1916</strong>, but Sgt. Morton of the
      10th Battalion, the only witness to the burial, says <strong>22 August, at Mouquet Farm</strong>.
      The 9th Battalion war diary for 20–22 August would settle it.</li>
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered same day</span> <strong>Vivian Claude Sneyd, MM and Bar,
      3rd Australian Field Ambulance</strong> (1895–1949) — Ivy's other brother. The archive did not
      know he had served at all. <strong>Found and read:</strong> his AWM 28 recommendation for the
      <strong>Bar</strong>, for Westhoek east of Ypres on 4 October 1917, and the front of his B2455
      dossier — No. <strong>1696</strong>, enlisted Brisbane 16 July 1915 aged 19, a salesman of
      Windsor, entitled to the 1914/15 Star, discharged 30 July 1919.
      <a href={u("/great-war")}>The citation →</a>
      <br /><strong>Still to do:</strong> the citation for the <strong>first</strong> Military Medal
      (awarded 1 March 1917 — the action is not yet identified), the other seventeen pages of his
      dossier, and <span class="mono">NAA J26, M17540</span>, his repatriation medical case file,
      which runs <strong>1915 to 1948</strong> and would say what the war did to him.
      <br />One correction it forces: his B.103 gives his birthplace as <strong>Windsor,
      Brisbane</strong>, where the tree says Rockhampton.</li>
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered same day</span> <strong>A third Sneyd soldier</strong> —
      <em>Pte Ernest Vivian William Sneyd, 3891</em>, 31st Battalion. Found and read:
      <span class="mono">NAA B2455, item 8087517</span>, ninety-two pages. His attestation names his
      father as <strong>Thomas George Sneyd of Landsborough</strong>, which confirms him as Arthur and
      Vivian's <strong>first cousin</strong> — the identification is no longer inferred. A labourer of
      25, once rejected for height, who enlisted 4 December 1915, was wounded with the 31st Battalion
      at the end of September 1917, invalided to England with bronchitis that November, admitted with
      <strong>albuminuria</strong> at Birmingham in May 1918, and sent home on a hospital ship on
      5 January 1919. <a href={u("/great-war")}>His service →</a>
      <br /><strong>Still to do:</strong> his <strong>Queensland death certificate</strong>. The tree
      says only "1919". The dossier shows eight months in hospital with kidney disease immediately
      before he sailed, and the certificate would say whether that is what killed him — which in turn
      decides whether he belongs on a memorial. Add him to the BDM order already drafted.</li>
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered</span> <strong>His death, 10 November 1936.</strong> The
      <em>Courier-Mail</em> obituary of 11 November has been found: he died <em>suddenly</em>, aged
      44, of Botany Street, Clayfield, and was buried at Toowong the same afternoon. The medical
      cause is still not established — that needs the Queensland death certificate.
      <a href={u("/papers")}>The obituary →</a></li>
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered</span> <strong>How George Pitt D'Arcy died, 21 March
      1931.</strong> Found dead in the kitchen of his home in Victoria Street, Banyo; inquest
      reported 24 April; his son gave evidence of untreated heart trouble. <strong>And the age is
      settled:</strong> the South Brisbane Cemetery record gives his birth as 5 May 1864, so he was
      <strong>66</strong> and the newspaper's "76" is an error.
      <a href={u("/papers")}>The report →</a></li>
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered</span> <strong>When did Frederick Robert D'Arcy die?</strong>
      <strong>19 September 1875</strong>, at his residence, Spring Hill, aged 63 — his family's death
      notice in the <em>Brisbane Courier</em> two days later. The Toowong monumental-inscription
      transcript, which says 10 September and "9th Regiment", is wrong on both counts.</li>
      <li><span class="chip doc">half answered</span> <strong>Constantine D'Arcy — born Barbados,
      died Chatham.</strong> The <em>birth</em> is now documented: christened <strong>St Michael,
      Barbados, 18 February 1786</strong>, father Robert Darcy, mother Jane. Connolly says
      <strong>"Died at Chatham, 6 5 1805"</strong>. So the tree is right about where he was born and
      wrong about where he died. <strong>Still to do:</strong> the Chatham burial entry itself, which
      would confirm it and give an age.</li>
      <li><span class="chip lore">searched, not found</span> <strong>An obituary for Robert
      D'Arcy.</strong> The <em>Gentleman's Magazine</em> for May, June and July 1827 has been searched
      in full: <strong>no notice of his death</strong>. Its 1827 volumes do carry a death notice for
      <strong>D'Arcy Wentworth</strong>, the colonial surgeon of New South Wales who died two months
      later — an unrelated man who will contaminate any search not watching for him.</li>
      <li><strong>Florence Isabel D'Arcy, died 22 August 1918</strong>, cause given as
      <em>chlorosis; valvular disease of the heart</em>, aged 24. Confirm the Nundah burial: the
      council record reads <em>F J D'Arcy</em> and the initial is assumed to be a mistranscription
      of <em>I</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>The 'This Is Your Life' book</strong> presented to Ken D'Arcy on his retirement from
      National Mutual in 1982, referred to in his eulogy. If it survives in the family it is a
      primary source for thirty-two years of his working life.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>4 · The families they married</h2>
    <ul class="dek">
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered in part</span> <strong>Atkinson, of Limerick.</strong> The
      christening of 9 July 1832 names his parents: <strong>Richard Atkinson and Anne</strong> — a
      generation gained. <strong>Still to do:</strong> anything at all about Richard and Anne, and
      the ship of 1862. Limerick is hard: many Church of Ireland registers burned in the Four Courts
      in 1922. <a href={u("/atkinson")}>The Atkinsons →</a></li>
      <li><span class="chip doc">answered in part</span> <strong>Murdoch, of St Quivox.</strong> The
      Scottish statutory register gives William Murdoch's birth as <strong>7 October 1856 at
      St Quivox and Newton</strong>, parents <strong>Francis Murdoch and Marion Watt</strong>; and
      their marriage as <strong>6 May 1854 at Avondale, Lanarkshire</strong> — Strathaven's parish,
      and a year later than the tree says. <strong>Still to do:</strong> the emigration to
      Queensland, and Francis's own parents. <a href={u("/murdoch")}>The Murdochs →</a></li>
      <li><strong>Matson, of Cornacreeve, County Monaghan.</strong> Catherine Jane Matson, born 1862,
      died Toowoomba 1914. Her parents William Matson and Mary Abbot have no dates at all.</li>
      <li><strong>Atwell.</strong> James Atwell is said to have <em>come to Australia in the merchant
      navy and deserted</em>. A desertion would appear in a crew agreement or a shipping-office
      record, and would date and name the ship. <span class="chip inf">probable</span> The 1861
      census has a <strong>James Atwell, aged 7, born Hammersmith</strong>, living at Westcombe
      Street, <strong>Batcombe, Somerset</strong> (Shepton Mallet district) as a <em>nephew</em> of
      the head of household, with Maria (5), Fred (3) and Henry (1) — a Somerset family with a
      London-born eldest, which is the right shape. Not yet proved to be him.</li>
      <li><strong>Blum.</strong> John Blum, born Germany 1842, reached Rockhampton. Where in Germany,
      and on what ship? His daughter Martha's dates are now confirmed — born 25 July 1870, died
      19 December 1904, buried Lutwyche — but he himself is still only a country and a year.</li>
      <li><strong>Sneyd — largely closed.</strong> The scepticism this page called for has now been
      applied, and it paid twice. The descent from <strong>Keele Hall is disproved</strong> and the
      real Madeley line found in its place; and the <strong>1902 coronial file</strong> confirms the
      Australian join under oath — William Hartley Sneyd's widow names his father as "Samuel Sneyd,
      Gaol Governor". His birth is now documented outright: the Queensland death registration
      <span class="mono">1902/B/2776</span> gives <strong>30 September 1837</strong>. This archive
      briefly published 1838 against it and was wrong — a stated age of 64 on 11 September, nineteen
      days before his birthday, is exactly what 1837 predicts.
      <a href={u("/inquest-1902")}>The file →</a> · <a href={u("/how-far-back")}>The broken graft →</a>
      <br /><strong>Samuel Charles Sneyd's own service record — searched again, 9 September 2026,
      and still not found.</strong> The Queensland Police <em>Staff Files</em>
      (<span class="mono">S1108</span>) hold two Sneyds and neither can be him: AF1623 runs 1881–83
      and AF1599 runs 1890–97, and he died in 1885. On dates they are two of his sons.
      <span class="chip lore">null result</span> He served before the Queensland force existed —
      NSW Mounted Police, then chief constable at Moreton Bay from 1849 — so his record, if it
      survives, is in a colonial series that predates S1108 or on the gaol side rather than the police
      side. The <strong>Queensland Blue Books</strong> would at least confirm his tenure and salary
      year by year and have not yet been tried.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

